cdk: don't reset the applied cursor on leaves caused by grabs

The native crossing events that a client receives when another client
grabs or releases the pointer are only ignored when the client has a
grab of its own; when it has none they are handled, and a leave caused
by a grab of another client clears the window under the pointer and
resets the applied cursor.  Since all cursors are applied on the
toplevel, undefining it leaves the whole window hierarchy without a
cursor on the server, and until the following enter event is processed
the server walks up the ancestors to the window manager frame windows,
whose cursor flashes over the client area; a pointer grab taken by the
other client in that instant freezes the frame cursor instead of the
cursor the pointer was showing.  Holding F10 on an app while the
pointer sits on another app's text box makes the menu grabs flicker the
frame cursor over the text box on every menu toggle.

Restrict the reset to leaves not caused by grabs: the applied cursor is
not visible while the other client's grab lasts, and it is what the
server shows again as soon as the grab ends, so keeping it defined
removes the flash and keeps the frame cursor from being frozen by grabs
taken in the meantime.
